+## 2026-09-08 Performance adoption
+
+The external canary now uses its ticket primary index and a transactional audit
+high-water counter. Shunter supplies invocation-scoped recovered table lookup,
+preserves sparse sidecar cadence across durable batches, and buffers snapshot
+file writes independently of hashing. The [implementation checklist](../working-docs/performance-implementation-progress.md)
+tracks maintained tests and the bounded adoption comparison.
+
+The [adoption comparison](../working-docs/performance-implementation-data/README.md)
+retained 16 workload samples: median writes/s 100.9 → 760.2 at eight clients and
+87.56 → 2,510 at 32. Matched completion was 13.533 → 0.491 s; shared-host control
+variation limits the speedup estimate. Fixed 32-client read p95/p99 rose
+0.4978/2.069 → 1.129/2.935 ms; matched tails rose 0.9290/2.830 →
+1.744/3.593 ms. At 100 operations/s they fell 0.8717/2.638 → 0.6067/1.560 ms.
+Ten separate large snapshot samples gave 74.82 → 65.93 ms median with 4,176 bytes
+extra allocation; one buffered sample was slower (85.68 ms). All exact-state,
+delivery and publication checks passed. These results add development evidence
+without establishing product latency acceptance.
+
+The [September 7 final validation](../working-docs/performance-investigation-data/final-validation-20260907/README.md)
+measured the original **canary plus runtime** against the four-patch candidate,
+without snapshot buffering: eight-client writes/s 117.45 → 785.30; 32-client
+writes/s 116.35 → 2,644.50. A matched 1,024-write job including two snapshots and
+confirmed delivery drain took 8.783 → 0.464 seconds and allocated 27.536 GiB →
+178.841 MiB on the server. These are historical workload results, not runtime-only
+attribution or acceptance thresholds; do not multiply isolated gains into them.
+
+Unpaced 32-client read p95 worsened from 0.358 to 0.852 ms, and from 0.367 to
+1.397 ms with matched history. At the same offered 100 operations/s it improved
+from 0.3375 to 0.2939 ms. Paced equal-content sampled RSS fell 230.8 → 179.2 MiB,
+while retained heap changed only 62.02 → 61.89 MiB. Faster fixed-duration runs
+retained more audits and used more memory. Medians of per-run percentiles are
+not pooled tails; sampled peaks can miss brief peaks. FullUpdate reply latency
+is not DurableSuccess latency. Product read-latency acceptance remains
+unspecified: choose a budget in milliseconds for the intended load.
+
+Sparse sidecars still sync once per durable batch. Fewer dirty writes improve
+throughput, but the larger native-index recovery study measured 3.267 → 3.645 ms
+and 0.860 → 1.015 MiB allocated. Existing full indexes are not resampled, and
+capacity exhaustion removes the normal sparse hint scan bound. Recovery timing
+excludes later full active-segment validation for append and complete hosted
+readiness. Segment defaults and compaction policy remain unchanged.
+
+File-only buffering is a separate, limited publication improvement. The
+[interleaved snapshot study](../working-docs/performance-investigation-data/continuation-20260907/README.md)
+measured large idle snapshots at 92.09 → 82.35 ms with about 4.8 KiB additional
+allocation. Small-snapshot and general hosted gains were inconclusive; no process
+memory improvement was established. Buffering the combined file/hash stream
+remains rejected because of its allocation regression.
 
